EXPECTED RED: `POST /v1/media = 404, want 201` — the mux's own 404, inside the JSON envelope, because httpx.MuxErrors already brings it there. That is the right reason. A red naming `undefined: media.FixtureHeroMountain` means R1 was skipped.

OTHER LEGS: commit before upload is 409 `upload_incomplete`; a contentType outside the allowlist is 422 and the SCHEMA refuses it too (a direct INSERT reddens); byteSize over the bound is 422 BEFORE anything is signed; mint answers one URL per id in order; mint of an uncommitted id is refused; every capability response carries both headers; a second begin for the same digest answers alreadyExists true and does NOT re-mint an upload URL for an object already there. THE SHARED-OBJECT LEG, which is the one content addressing exists for: two photographs referencing one object both survive the deletion of either, because there is exactly one row and the reference count is the four foreign keys.

MUTATIONS: drop the committed-asset check (the negative half reddens); make it reject everything (the POSITIVE half reddens — this is the mutation that proves the leg is not the vacuous one v6 warned about); drop `no-store` (the header leg reddens); derive alreadyExists from row existence rather than uploaded_at (the begin-twice leg reddens).

TEST-FIRST, and the first leg is the strongest one this project can write.

```go
// THE WHOLE-LOG ROUND TRIP THROUGH POSTGRESQL. VS7 proved the client's own
// document round-trips through internal/logbook's TYPES; nothing has ever put
// a city, a place, a visit, a photograph or a walk into PostgreSQL and read it
// back through the emitter. So the six read queries' column ordering, the
// visits nesting and the jsonb_array_elements unnest are today guarded by
// their scans compiling and by nothing else.
//
// The reference was not written beside the code that has to satisfy it, and
// that is the whole of its strength: it proves every key, every date string,
// every number and every ORDER BY at once, against bytes neither this package
// nor its tests authored.
func TestTheClientsOwnLogSurvivesPostgreSQL(t *testing.T) {
	db, travellerID := freshTraveller(t)

	raw, err := os.ReadFile("../logbook/testdata/client_sample_log.json")
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	want, err := logbook.DecodeEnvelope(raw) // the client's bytes, unmodified
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("the reference document no longer decodes: %v", err)
	}

	// The ONLY transformation. Everything else must survive untouched.
	want.Logbook, err = logbook.RewriteAssets(want.Logbook, media.FixtureDigests)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("RewriteAssets: %v", err)
	}

	if err := seed.Load(t.Context(), db, seed.FromDocument(travellerID, want.Logbook), seed.LoadOptions{}); err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("Load: %v", err)
	}

	var got logbook.Document
	store := postgres.LogbookStore{DB: db}
	snap, err := store.Read(t.Context(), travellerID, func(int64) bool { return true })
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("Read: %v", err)
	}
	got = *snap.Document

	envelope, err := logbook.Emit(logbook.FormatVersion, got)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatalf("Emit: %v", err)
	}

	if diff := cmp.Diff(want.Logbook, envelope.Logbook); diff != "" {
		t.Errorf("the log did not survive a round trip through PostgreSQL (-client +server):\n%s", diff)
	}

	// Named separately because the diff above cannot say WHICH rule broke, and
	// this one rebinds what the user sees: the client reads visits.first.at as
	// "last visited", so a place whose visits come back oldest-first shows the
	// wrong date on P1 with every field individually correct.
	for _, p := range envelope.Logbook.Places {
		for i := 1; i < len(p.Visits); i++ {
			if p.Visits[i].At.After(p.Visits[i-1].At) {
				t.Fatalf("place %s: visits came back oldest-first at index %d (%s after %s) — "+
					"the client reads visits.first.at as lastVisited",
					p.ID, i, p.Visits[i].At, p.Visits[i-1].At)
			}
		}
	}
}
```

EXPECTED RED: `undefined: seed.FromDocument` / `seed: Load is not implemented`. Both are the right reason. A red that is a `cmp.Diff` on the very first run means somebody implemented before writing this, which §8's backfill rule says must be LABELLED as a backfill and proved by breaking the subject instead.

OTHER LEGS: `make seed` twice — the second refuses and says so, and the database is UNCHANGED afterwards (assert a row count, not the exit code); RewriteAssets refuses an unmapped locator and names it; two reads with no write between them are BYTE-IDENTICAL, which is the ETag's whole promise and which the slice could only test against one trip; the read's 85,422 bytes go through the real handler and the response size is recorded (the performance lens's first real number); a seeded traveller's first GET carries a valid ETag rather than none.

MUTATIONS: drop `ORDER BY ordinal, id` from readVisits (the ordering leg reddens, and the round trip reddens too — note WHICH, because two legs reddening from one mutation is one assertion wearing two names unless they say different things); drop `ORDER BY ordinal` from readTripCities (cityIds comes back in a different order); assign visit ordinals in reverse in FromDocument (the ordering leg reddens and the round-trip diff names `at`); point one locator at the other's digest (the diff names `asset` on 189 photographs, which is a satisfying red and also the argument for RewriteAssets being a named function).

TEST-FIRST. The failing test is the share reset, because it is the one where a wrong answer is a privacy leak rather than a bug:

```go
// THE CRITIC CALLED THIS LEG EXACTLY RIGHT AT v1 AND IT IS CARRIED VERBATIM:
// turn coordinates ON, stop sharing, mint a NEW link, and assert the new link
// does not carry coordinates. Removing the reset is a privacy leak, not a
// tidiness issue — the next link hands out exact pins without anybody having
// turned that on.
//
// AND THE RESET IS NOT "the column defaults". Migration 0002 moved the
// DEFAULTs to the client's true/true/false, but a DEFAULT does not reach an
// UPDATE, so an implementation that leans on it resets nothing at all. The
// three values are asserted BY NAME.
func TestStoppingSharingDisarmsTheSwitchesForTheNextLink(t *testing.T) {
	srv := newAPI(t)
	token := registerAndSignIn(t, srv)
	putJSON(t, srv, token, "/v1/trips/kyoto", map[string]any{"name": "Kyoto in May"})

	if r := putJSON(t, srv, token, "/v1/trips/kyoto/share", map[string]any{
		"shareCoordinates": true,
	}); r.Status != 200 {
		t.Fatalf("PUT share = %d, want 200", r.Status)
	}

	if r := deleteReq(t, srv, token, "/v1/trips/kyoto/share"); r.Status != 200 {
		t.Fatalf("DELETE share = %d, want 200", r.Status)
	}

	minted := postJSON(t, srv, token, "/v1/trips/kyoto/share", map[string]any{
		"token": "mnpqrstuvwxy",
	})
	if minted.Status != 201 {
		t.Fatalf("POST share = %d, want 201", minted.Status)
	}

	trip := minted.Body
	if trip["shareCoordinates"] != false {
		t.Errorf("shareCoordinates = %v on a NEW link after stopping, want false — "+
			"a killed link must not leave a coordinate switch armed for the next one",
			trip["shareCoordinates"])
	}
	// The other two go the OTHER way, and that asymmetry is the client's:
	// stopSharing writes Trip.defaultSharePhotos and defaultShareNotes, both true.
	if trip["sharePhotos"] != true {
		t.Errorf("sharePhotos = %v, want true — stopSharing resets to the client's "+
			"defaults, and an implementation leaning on the column DEFAULT resets "+
			"nothing, because a DEFAULT does not reach an UPDATE", trip["sharePhotos"])
	}
	if trip["shareNotes"] != true {
		t.Errorf("shareNotes = %v, want true", trip["shareNotes"])
	}
	if trip["shareLinkId"] != "mnpqrstuvwxy" {
		t.Errorf("shareLinkId = %v, want the token the client supplied", trip["shareLinkId"])
	}
}
```

EXPECTED RED: `PUT share = 404, want 200`, the mux's 404 inside the envelope.

D3's LEGS, one per row of the sheet's own table, EACH ASSERTING ON THE SURVIVING ROW COUNT and never on error/no-error (DBA F2): 'N photos and their notes' go; 'N recorded walks' go; 'N pins in …' are KEPT — including the one left with zero visits; the cities are untouched; the link stops working; and ANOTHER trip's photograph that named a visit on the deleted trip comes back with visit_id NULL and everything else unchanged. That last one is the cascade the client's own history says moved no count and left the log corrupt.

OTHER LEGS: stop-then-new-link succeeds, which is the exact sequence that fails outright under the primary key nobody specified (DEC-67); two live links for one trip are refused by share_links_one_live; the revocation record survives, so history shows which token was live when; a revoked session token is a 401 on the next request; PATCH /v1/me with an empty name is 422 and does not clear it, matching the client, which has no way to clear a traveller's name.

MUTATIONS: remove the flag reset (the leg above reddens on shareCoordinates); reset to the schema DEFAULT instead of writing the three values (reddens on sharePhotos and shareNotes and NOT on shareCoordinates — which is the mutation that proves DEC-80 and the reset are two different mechanisms); delete a trip with `DELETE FROM places WHERE id IN (SELECT place_id FROM visits WHERE trip_id = $1)`, the CRUD-by-reflex version (the wishlist-place leg reddens, and this is the mutation v6 named as reachable and never ran); skip the revoke on POST share (the one-live index raises instead, so the leg must assert the 201 and the row count, not the absence of an error).

TEST-FIRST. The failing test is D2's delete branch, because it is the one an ordering mistake makes silently wrong:

```go
// D2's DELETE BRANCH, AND IT IS ABOUT THE ORDER OF TWO STATEMENTS.
// The sheet says "all N, and the notes you wrote on them". Delete the place
// first and photos_place_fk clears place_id on every one of them, so the
// DELETE that follows matches nothing and the photographs survive — with the
// user having explicitly asked for them to go, and no error anywhere.
//
// ASSERT ON THE SURVIVING ROW COUNT, NEVER ON ERROR/NO-ERROR (DBA F2): before
// DEC-66's column-list SET NULL the wrong order raised a NOT NULL violation
// instead, so a leg written against an error would have reddened for the wrong
// reason, which DEC-28 forbids.
func TestRemovingAPlaceAndItsPhotographsActuallyRemovesThem(t *testing.T) {
	srv, db := newAPIWithSeed(t) // the client's own log, 284 photographs
	token := signInAsTheSeededTraveller(t, srv)

	const place = "fushimi-inari"
	before := countPhotos(t, db, place)
	if before == 0 {
		t.Fatalf("the fixture holds no photographs at %s — this leg would pass vacuously", place)
	}
	walksBefore := countWalks(t, db)

	r := deleteReq(t, srv, token, "/v1/places/"+place+"?photos=delete")
	if r.Status != 200 {
		t.Fatalf("DELETE = %d %v, want 200 with the whole logbook", r.Status, r.Body)
	}

	if after := countPhotos(t, db, place); after != 0 {
		t.Errorf("%d of %d photographs filed at %s survived a ?photos=delete — "+
			"the place was almost certainly deleted BEFORE them, so "+
			"ON DELETE SET NULL (place_id) cleared their pin and the DELETE "+
			"matched nothing", after, before, place)
	}
	if after := countWalks(t, db); after != walksBefore {
		t.Errorf("walks moved from %d to %d — D2 says the track stays with the day "+
			"it was recorded either way, and there is no place_id on walks at all",
			walksBefore, after)
	}

	// The parameter is REQUIRED. A default is a silent answer to the question
	// the sheet makes the user answer.
	if r := deleteReq(t, srv, token, "/v1/places/tofuku-ji"); r.Status != 422 ||
		r.Body["field"] != "photos" {
		t.Errorf("DELETE with no ?photos = %d %v, want 422 invalid_field on photos",
			r.Status, r.Body)
	}
}
```

EXPECTED RED: `DELETE = 404, want 200`. Note the `before == 0` guard: without it, a leg run against an unseeded database passes while asserting nothing, and this project has shipped that shape before.

D2's OTHER THREE PROMISES: on keep, the photographs survive with place_id AND visit_id both NULL and their date and city UNCHANGED (all four asserted, because a mutation clearing only one of the two would otherwise pass); on both branches the walks are untouched; the visits go with the pin either way, and the SHEET SAYS SO because nothing could have kept them.

OTHER LEGS: a city created with attachTo appears in that trip's cityIds AT THE END of the ordered list, and the response is the WHOLE logbook; a city created without attachTo belongs to no trip and the response is the city; a place whose visits array is re-sent in a different order comes back in the new order (this is the delete-then-insert reorder, and it is the first time the non-deferrable UNIQUE on visits ordinals is exercised by a route); deleting a city that has places is refused by places_city_fk with a 409, which is DEC-57 being visible at exactly the moment somebody would need to write the sheet copy.

MUTATIONS: REORDER the delete branch to drop the place first — the leg above reddens naming the surviving count, and this is the two-line statement swap DEC-28 exists for; default ?photos to keep (the required-parameter leg reddens); clear place_id but not visit_id on the keep branch (the four-field leg reddens on visitId only); append the attached city at the START of cityIds (the ordering leg reddens).

